\chapter{The Core Module}
\label{scholar-module}

This chapter establishes the foundational abstractions upon which all of
Scholar is built.
Before we can search databases, cache results, or review papers, we need a
common vocabulary: what \emph{is} a paper?
What does a search result contain?
How do we represent queries in a way that works across databases with
different capabilities?

The answers to these questions shape every subsequent chapter.
A paper from Semantic Scholar must be comparable to one from DBLP for
deduplication to work.
A search result must capture enough metadata for reproducible reporting.
Get these abstractions wrong, and complexity propagates throughout the system;
get them right, and the rest follows naturally.

We define three core abstractions:
\begin{description}
\item[Paper] The fundamental unit of scholarly output---a title, authors, year,
  and optional metadata like abstract, DOI, and venue.
  Papers from different sources are unified through this common representation.
\item[SearchResult] A timestamped collection of papers from a single provider,
  preserving the provenance needed for reproducible documentation.
\item[Search] The orchestrator that coordinates queries across multiple
  providers, merges results, and applies filters.
\end{description}

The chapter proceeds as follows: we first establish the data model
(\cref{sec:data-model})---including [[SearchFilters]] for constraining
queries---then implement the [[Search]] class that coordinates queries across
providers.


\section{Overall design}

The system consists of several components:
\begin{description}
\item[Search providers] Adapters for different bibliographic databases
  (Semantic~Scholar, OpenAlex, IEEE~Xplore, Web of Science, DBLP, etc.).
\item[Query abstraction] A common query format that translates to
  provider-specific syntax.
\item[Results management] Data structures for papers, with deduplication across
  sources.
\item[Report generation] Tools to export structured, reproducible search
  documentation.
\end{description}

