\subsection{Representing papers}

A paper has bibliographic metadata that we need for deduplication and export.
Different databases return varying amounts of detail, so we design for the
common core while allowing optional fields:

<<classes>>=
@dataclass
class Paper:
    """Represents a paper from a bibliographic database."""

    title: str
    authors: list[str]
    year: int | None = None
    doi: str | None = None
    abstract: str | None = None
    venue: str | None = None
    url: str | None = None
    pdf_url: str | None = None
    citation_count: int | None = None
    keywords: list[str] | None = None
    tags: list[str] | None = None
    sources: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    references: list["Paper"] | None = None
    citations: list["Paper"] | None = None

    <<paper methods>>
@

Session JSON files sometimes contain [[null]] for list fields like
[[authors]] or [[sources]]---this happens when a provider returns incomplete
metadata.
Since [[dict.get("authors", [])]] returns [[None]] (not [[[]]]) when the key
is present but the value is [[null]], we enforce the list invariant in
[[__post_init__]] so that all downstream code can safely iterate and slice
without [[None]]-guards.

OpenAlex also returns [[null]] for [[title]] (e.g.\ journal issues and
editorials).  We coerce that to an empty string here so that string
operations such as slicing and [[.lower()]] never crash.

<<paper methods>>=
def __post_init__(self):
    """Coerce None list fields to empty lists and None title to ''."""
    if self.title is None:
        self.title = ""
    if self.authors is None:
        self.authors = []
    if self.sources is None:
        self.sources = []
@

