We use a dataclass for simplicity.
The [[citation_count]] field stores the number of citations if available from
the provider.
The [[keywords]] field stores author-provided keywords or index terms when a
provider exposes them.
The [[tags]] field stores provider-specific topical labels (e.g., OpenAlex
concepts or Semantic Scholar fields of study) as a fallback when author keywords
are not available.
The [[sources]] field tracks which databases returned this paper, useful for
provenance in reports when merging results from multiple providers.

\subsubsection{Keywords versus tags}

Different providers expose different 
\enquote{keyword-like} metadata.
Some databases preserve the authors' own terms, while others provide derived
topic labels.
We keep these separate so downstream consumers can prefer author intent when it
exists, while still benefiting from topical labels as a fallback.

As a concrete example, IEEE Xplore might provide author index terms like
[["threshold cryptography"]], while Semantic Scholar may only provide a broader
field label like [["Computer Science"]].

In general:
\begin{description}
\item[[[keywords]]] author-provided keywords or index terms.
\item[[[tags]]] provider-derived topical labels.
\end{description}

\subsubsection{Paper identity and deduplication}

When merging results from multiple databases, we need to identify duplicate
papers.
We use a two-tier identity system:
\begin{enumerate}
\item If both papers have a DOI, compare DOIs (case-insensitive).
\item Otherwise, compare normalized title combined with the first author's
  last name.
\end{enumerate}

This matches the identity used by the Paper Registry, ensuring that
[[paper1 == paper2]] if and only if they would map to the same registry entry.

The [[id]] property returns a stable string identifier for a paper.
This is used as the key in the Paper Registry and for persistent storage.

\paragraph{Performance optimization.}
The [[id]] property computes a SHA256 hash from the paper's title and first
author when no DOI is available. With large paper sessions (1000+ papers),
this property is accessed repeatedly during equality checks ([[__eq__]]),
hash operations ([[__hash__]]), notes lookup, and list rendering---resulting
in thousands of redundant hash computations.

Using [[functools.cached_property]] instead of [[property]] caches the computed
value as an instance attribute after the first access. Since [[Paper]] objects
are effectively immutable (their title, authors, and DOI don't change after
creation), this caching is safe and eliminates redundant computation.

<<paper methods>>=
@cached_property
def id(self) -> str:
    """
    Generate a stable identifier for this paper.

    Uses DOI if available, otherwise SHA256 hash of normalized
    title + first author's last name.

    Returns:
        A string identifier unique to this paper's identity.
    """
    if self.doi:
        return f"doi:{self.doi.lower()}"

    # Normalize title
    title_normalized = self.title.lower().strip()

    # Get first author's last name if available
    author_part = ""
    if self.authors:
        first_author = self.authors[0]
        # Extract last name (last word of author name)
        last_name = first_author.split()[-1].lower() if first_author else ""
        author_part = last_name

    # Create hash
    content = f"{title_normalized}|{author_part}"
    hash_value = hashlib.sha256(content.encode()).hexdigest()[:16]
    return f"hash:{hash_value}"

def __eq__(self, other: object) -> bool:
    """
    Check equality based on stable paper identity.

    Two papers are equal if they have the same DOI (case-insensitive),
    or if they have the same normalized title and first author's last name.
    """
    if not isinstance(other, Paper):
        return NotImplemented
    return self.id == other.id

def __hash__(self) -> int:
    """Hash based on stable paper identity."""
    return hash(self.id)
@

This allows us to use papers in sets for automatic deduplication.

\subsubsection{Serialization and deserialization}

For caching enrichment data (references and citations), we need to serialize
papers to dictionaries and deserialize them back.
The [[to_dict]] method converts a paper to a dictionary suitable for JSON
serialization or pickle storage.

By default, we exclude references and citations to avoid infinite recursion
when papers reference each other.
When [[include_refs_cites=True]], we serialize them one level deep (the
referenced papers themselves have their refs/cites excluded).

<<paper methods>>=
def to_dict(self, include_refs_cites: bool = False) -> dict:
    """
    Convert paper to a dictionary for serialization.

    Args:
        include_refs_cites: If True, include references and citations
            (serialized one level deep, without their own refs/cites).

    Returns:
        Dictionary representation of the paper.
    """
    result = {
        "title": self.title,
        "authors": self.authors,
        "year": self.year,
        "doi": self.doi,
        "abstract": self.abstract,
        "venue": self.venue,
        "url": self.url,
        "pdf_url": self.pdf_url,
        "citation_count": self.citation_count,
        "keywords": self.keywords,
        "tags": self.tags,
        "sources": self.sources,
    }

    if include_refs_cites:
        result["references"] = (
            [p.to_dict(include_refs_cites=False) for p in self.references]
            if self.references else None
        )
        result["citations"] = (
            [p.to_dict(include_refs_cites=False) for p in self.citations]
            if self.citations else None
        )

    return result
@

The [[from_dict]] class method reconstructs a Paper from a dictionary.
It handles both the old format (without refs/cites) and the new format
(with refs/cites as nested dictionaries).

By default, [[from_dict]] uses the Paper Registry to ensure each unique paper
is represented by a single object.
The [[use_registry]] parameter can disable this for special cases (like
creating temporary papers for comparison).

<<paper methods>>=
@classmethod
def from_dict(cls, data: dict, use_registry: bool = True) -> "Paper":
    """
    Create a Paper from a dictionary, using the registry for deduplication.

    Handles both legacy format (no refs/cites) and new format
    (refs/cites as list of dicts).

    Args:
        data: Dictionary with paper fields.
        use_registry: If True (default), use the Paper Registry to ensure
            each unique paper is represented by a single object.

    Returns:
        Paper instance (may be an existing paper from the registry).
    """
    if use_registry:
        return get_registry().get_or_create(data)
    return cls._from_dict_without_registry(data)

@classmethod
def _from_dict_without_registry(cls, data: dict) -> "Paper":
    """
    Create a Paper from a dictionary without using the registry.

    This is used internally by the registry's get_or_create method,
    and for cases where registry use should be bypassed.

    Args:
        data: Dictionary with paper fields.

    Returns:
        New Paper instance.
    """
    # Parse references if present (recursively, but without registry
    # to avoid infinite loops during get_or_create)
    references = None
    if "references" in data and data["references"] is not None:
        references = [cls._from_dict_without_registry(ref) for ref in data["references"]]

    # Parse citations if present
    citations = None
    if "citations" in data and data["citations"] is not None:
        citations = [cls._from_dict_without_registry(cit) for cit in data["citations"]]

    return cls(
        title=data.get("title", "") or "",
        authors=data.get("authors", []) or [],
        year=data.get("year"),
        doi=data.get("doi"),
        abstract=data.get("abstract"),
        venue=data.get("venue"),
        url=data.get("url"),
        pdf_url=data.get("pdf_url"),
        citation_count=data.get("citation_count"),
        keywords=data.get("keywords"),
        tags=data.get("tags"),
        sources=data.get("sources", []) or [],
        references=references,
        citations=citations,
    )
@

